## Steps

1. Write every exam, assignment deadline, class meeting, lab, office hour, and no-class day to
   {{run_dir}}/events.json as a JSON list with ONE EVENT PER LINE and no indentation, using a heredoc:
   cat <<'EOF' > {{run_dir}}/events.json
   [
   {"course": "<course>", "title": "<short name>", "type": "exam", "date": "YYYY-MM-DD", "start": "HH:MM", "end": "HH:MM", "recurrence": null, "citation": "p.N: '<exact words copied from the syllabus>'", "confidence": "high"},
   ...one line like that per event, with all nine fields...
   ]
   EOF
   Write ALL your events in this ONE command. Never add to the file afterwards with >>: a second
   piece makes the file invalid JSON.

   How to read a syllabus:
   - Class meetings come from the meeting line (e.g. "MWF 8:00-8:50", "TTh 9:30-10:50"): ONE event
     per meeting pattern with a weekly recurrence, not one event per lecture or per topic.
   - A schedule table of lecture topics (8.1, 8.2, "Intro", ...) is NOT a list of events. From it,
     take only exams, deadlines, and days with no class ("N/A", "no class", "holiday").
   - Each discussion/lab section is one weekly event.
   - Meeting days are written many ways: "TTh", "T/Th", "TR", "Tu/Th", "Tuesday and Thursday",
     "MWF", "M/W/F", "Mon-Fri". So are times: "9:30-10:50am", "2PM", "02:00 PM - 03:20 PM", "10-12".
   - The meeting line may be labeled "Class", "Lecture", "Schedule", "Meets", "Time and Place", or
     have no label. Every course meets; if you have no class event yet, read the top of page 1 again.
   - Office hours that say only "See Canvas" or "by appointment" have no time: skip them.
   - The last week of the term is finals week: weekly meetings end before it (UNTIL = the Sunday
     before it), and only final exams go in it.
   - If the syllabus says something happens but never says when (e.g. "5 in-class quizzes"), you
     may estimate the dates: spread them over the term on class days, skipping holidays. Every
     estimated event gets confidence "low"; cite the sentence that mentions it. The student
     confirms estimated dates later.
{%- if mode == "full" %}
2. Check your file:
   python -m sycal.validate {{run_dir}}/events.json --term-start {{term_start}} --term-end {{term_end}} --text {{run_dir}}/text.txt --fix
   (--fix repairs simple format slips itself and lists them as "auto-fixed"; you only fix the errors.)
   If it prints OK, go straight to step 3. If it prints FAIL, fix every error it lists by writing
   the WHOLE file again with the same cat <<'EOF' command (do not edit it with sed), then run the
   check once more. You get two checks; after that the run ends with the file as it is.
3. Submit: echo COMPLETE_TASK_AND_SUBMIT_FINAL_OUTPUT
